Actually, that's _really_ odd; the database shows 118 games for that player,
but there are 124 lines in the file.  Notice there are 6 extra lines for that
game, so those account for the discrepancy.

Chances are that the duplicate lines are from logfile synch processes that were
stopped before completing, since we had some trouble getting that process
optimized with so many extra entries from folks fishing for starting equipment;
at one point it was taking over 30 minutes to parse all the servers' logfiles
before we noticed it and came up with a more efficient way to guarantee order
and uniqueness in the database.

The servers.xlogfile is built seperately from the database, though, and
apparently with less checking to make sure it's accurate.  ;-)

(The database import function checks for the highest index currently in the
database, then skips to the next line after that index before reading in new
lines from the logfile.  So the database shouldn't be able to get duplicate
entries.)

That file is a backup, at least, and the scoreboard is calculated only from the
database.

If you look at the file, by the way, all those lines have the same
"server-index" tag at the beginning ("2-14168", meaning it was game number
14168 on server number 2), so any of our stuff reading that file would know to
ignore the duplicates.  I think I never mentioned that addition to Aardvark
Joe's file format, though, so eit_krog wouldn't have known to use that field as
the unique per game tag.

